      "text": "To find the expression for \\(f^{-1}\\), we start by examining the function \\(f = q \\circ p \\circ r\\). The function \\(f(x)\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ f(x) = q(p(r(x))). \\]\n\nTo find the inverse function \\(f^{-1}(x)\\), we need to express \\(x\\) in terms of \\(f^{-1}(x)\\). Since \\(f(x) = q(p(r(x)))\\), we can write:\n\n\\[ y = f(x) = q(p(r(x))). \\]\n\nTo find \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\), we need to reverse the operations of \\(f\\). First, we apply the inverse of \\(q\\), which is \\(q^{-1}\\):\n\n\\[ q^{-1}(y) = x. \\]\n\nNext, we apply the inverse of \\(p\\), which is \\(p^{-1}\\):\n\n\\[ p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y)) = r(x). \\]\n\nFinally, we apply the inverse of \\(r\\), which is \\(r^{-1}\\):\n\n\\[ r^{-1}(p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y))) = x. \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[ r^{-1}(p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y))) = x. \\]\n\nRewriting this in the form \\(f^{-1}(x)\\), we get:\n\n\\[ f^{-1}(x) = r^{-1}(p^{-1}(q^{-1}(x))). \\]\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for \\(f^{-1}\\) is:\n\n\\[ r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}. \\]\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{C}\\).",
